---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

interface Row {
  format: string;
  lossy: string;
  typical: string;
  support: string;
  use: string;
}

const rows: Row[] = [
  {
    format: 'MP3',
    lossy: 'Lossy',
    typical: '1 MB/min at 128 kbps',
    support: 'Universal',
    use: 'Anything that has to play everywhere, on anything, including a 2006 car stereo.',
  },
  {
    format: 'AAC / M4A',
    lossy: 'Lossy',
    typical: '0.9 MB/min at 128 kbps',
    support: 'Very wide',
    use: 'Better than MP3 at the same bitrate. The default for Apple devices and YouTube.',
  },
  {
    format: 'Opus',
    lossy: 'Lossy',
    typical: '0.5 MB/min at 64 kbps',
    support: 'Modern browsers, Discord, WhatsApp',
    use: 'The best quality per byte by a wide margin, especially for speech. Poor legacy hardware support.',
  },
  {
    format: 'OGG Vorbis',
    lossy: 'Lossy',
    typical: '1 MB/min at 128 kbps',
    support: 'Wide except Apple',
    use: 'Royalty-free. Standard in game engines like Unity and Godot.',
  },
  {
    format: 'WMA',
    lossy: 'Lossy',
    typical: '1 MB/min at 128 kbps',
    support: 'Windows-centric, declining',
    use: 'Legacy only. Convert away from it rather than to it.',
  },
  {
    format: 'FLAC',
    lossy: 'Lossless',
    typical: '5 MB/min',
    support: 'Wide except older Apple',
    use: 'Archiving and distribution where quality must be bit-perfect but size still matters.',
  },
  {
    format: 'ALAC',
    lossy: 'Lossless',
    typical: '5.5 MB/min',
    support: 'Apple ecosystem',
    use: 'FLAC’s equivalent for people living inside Apple software.',
  },
  {
    format: 'WAV',
    lossy: 'Uncompressed',
    typical: '10 MB/min at 44.1 kHz stereo',
    support: 'Universal',
    use: 'Editing and mastering. Never for distribution. It is ten times the size for no audible gain.',
  },
  {
    format: 'AIFF',
    lossy: 'Uncompressed',
    typical: '10 MB/min',
    support: 'Universal',
    use: 'The Mac world’s WAV. Functionally interchangeable.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Audio format guide: which one should you use?',
    description:
      'MP3, AAC, Opus, FLAC, WAV and the rest compared by size, quality, compatibility and what each is actually for.',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
    publisher: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'All tools', item: SITE.url },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Audio format guide',
        item: new URL('/audio-formats', SITE.url).href,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Audio Format Guide: MP3 vs AAC vs FLAC vs WAV vs Opus"
  description="Every common audio format compared: size per minute, quality, device support, and what each is genuinely best at. Plus what bitrate is actually enough."
  path="/audio-formats"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom: var(--s10)">
      <h1>Which audio format should you use?</h1>
      <p class="toolhead__sub">
        Nine formats, what each is actually for, and the two rules that settle
        most decisions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tablewrap" style="margin-bottom: var(--s12)">
      <table class="datatable">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Format</th>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Size</th>
            <th scope="col">Support</th>
            <th scope="col" style="white-space: normal; min-width: 24rem">Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" style="font-weight: 600">
                  {row.format}
                </th>
                <td>{row.lossy}</td>
                <td>{row.typical}</td>
                <td style="white-space: normal">{row.support}</td>
                <td style="white-space: normal">{row.use}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The two rules that settle most decisions</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Edit in lossless, distribute in lossy.</strong> Every time you
        decode and re-encode a lossy file you lose a little more, the way a
        photocopy of a photocopy degrades. Keep WAV or FLAC while you are
        working, and encode to MP3 or AAC exactly once, at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick for the worst device that has to play it.</strong> Opus is
        technically the best lossy codec available, but if the file needs to
        play on a ten-year-old car stereo, its technical superiority is
        irrelevant. MP3 remains the correct answer more often than audio people
        like to admit.
      </p>

      <h2>What bitrate is actually enough?</h2>
      <p>
        For music in MP3, 192 kbps is where most listeners stop being able to
        pick the compressed file out of a pair in a blind test, and 256 kbps is
        comfortably transparent for nearly everyone. 320 kbps exists mostly for
        peace of mind. Below 128 kbps you start hearing it on cymbals and
        applause first. Dense high-frequency noise is what MP3 handles worst.
      </p>
      <p>
        For speech, the numbers drop dramatically. A voice recording at 64 kbps
        mono AAC sounds fine, and Opus at 32 kbps is genuinely usable. A podcast
        does not need 192 kbps stereo, and shipping one at that rate triples
        your hosting bill for something nobody can hear.
      </p>
      <p>
        AAC buys roughly a 20–30% bitrate advantage over MP3 at equivalent
        quality; Opus roughly doubles it again at low bitrates. So 128 kbps AAC
        is about the same as 160–192 kbps MP3, and 64 kbps Opus beats both for
        voice.
      </p>

      <h2>Sample rate and bit depth</h2>
      <p>
        44.1 kHz is the CD standard and covers everything humans can hear. Sampling theory means it reproduces frequencies up to 22 kHz, which is
        already above most adults' hearing. 48 kHz is the video and broadcast
        standard. Higher rates matter during production, where processing
        artefacts can fold back into the audible range, but they buy nothing in
        a finished file.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bit depth controls dynamic range, not fidelity: 16-bit gives 96 dB, more
        than any listening environment can use, while 24-bit gives 144 dB and
        exists so engineers can record with generous headroom and normalize
        later without amplifying noise. Deliver 16-bit; work in 24-bit or float.
      </p>

      <h2>The formats to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Do not distribute WAV. It is ten times the size of a transparent MP3 and
        nobody can hear the difference on a finished master. Do not convert
        <em>to</em> WMA in 2026 unless a specific device demands it. And do not
        convert a lossy file to FLAC expecting improvement, you get a lossless
        copy of the damage that was already done, at five times the size.
      </p>
      <p>
        Converting between formats is free of extra loss only when the source is
        lossless. If you have an MP3 and need an M4A, you will lose a small
        amount; there is no way around that, and it is usually inaudible for a
        single conversion at a decent bitrate.
      </p>

      <h2>Convert between any of these</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/audio-converter">audio converter</a> handles every format
        on this page, in your browser, with nothing uploaded. MP3 and WAV are
        instant; the rest load a converter once and then stay fast. If your goal
        is a smaller file rather than a different format, the
        <a href="/audio-compressor">file size reducer</a> shows the projected
        size before you commit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
